---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { bySlug, destMonthUrl, deg, MONTHS } from '../../lib/data';

const g = bySlug.get('galapagos')!;
const calendar: string[] = [
  'Warm season begins in earnest. Green sea turtles nest on the beaches and land iguanas mate on Isabela. Calm seas and warm water make it great for snorkelling.',
  'Greater flamingos nest on Floreana and marine iguanas nest on Santa Cruz. Water is around its warmest, and seas are calm.',
  'The wettest and hottest month, with short tropical showers and lush green islands. Waved albatrosses start arriving on Española late in the month.',
  'Waved albatross courtship begins on Española — one of the archipelago’s great sights. Green turtle eggs and land iguana eggs hatch.',
  'Blue-footed boobies perform their courtship dances on North Seymour and Española. Albatrosses lay their eggs.',
  'The cool garúa season starts. Humpback whales arrive, and giant tortoises on Santa Cruz migrate to the lowlands to nest.',
  'Seabirds are very active: blue-footed boobies nest and flightless cormorants court on Fernandina. Whales and dolphins feed in the west.',
  'Sea lion pupping begins, and Nazca boobies and swallow-tailed gulls nest. The coolest water and roughest seas of the year.',
  'Peak of the cool season. Sea lions are very active and Galápagos penguins court on Bartolomé.',
  'Fur seals mate and blue-footed boobies raise their chicks. Giant tortoises are still laying eggs.',
  'Playful sea lion pups make for memorable snorkelling. Seas start to calm and the water begins to warm.',
  'Giant tortoise eggs start hatching (through April) and green sea turtles mate. Young albatrosses leave Española. The warm season returns.',
];
---

<Guide slug="galapagos-month-by-month" photo="galapagos">
  <p>
    There’s no bad time to visit the Galápagos: the wildlife is there all year and remarkably unafraid of people. What changes
    is the weather, the sea and which animals are nesting, courting or raising young. The islands have two seasons:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><b>December–May, the warm season:</b> hotter, sunnier and calmer, with short tropical showers, warm water (often 25–28°C) and the best snorkelling visibility.</li>
    <li><b>June–November, the cool ‘garúa’ season:</b> mistier skies, cooler water (around 22–24°C, colder at depth and in the west) and rougher seas — but nutrient-rich currents bring more marine life, from whales to penguins.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Galápagos weather and water temperature by month</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="data-table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Galápagos climate by month</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Month</th><th scope="col">Rating</th><th scope="col">Air high / low</th><th scope="col">Sea</th><th scope="col">Rain</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          g.months.map((m) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row"><a href={destMonthUrl(g, m.index)}>{m.name}</a></th>
              <td><span class="badge" data-rating={m.rating}>{m.score}</span></td>
              <td>{deg(m.high)} / {deg(m.low)}</td>
              <td>{m.sea !== null ? deg(m.sea) : '—'}</td>
              <td>{m.rain} mm</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="small muted">Air temperatures from official climate normals; sea-surface temperatures are 2023–2025 averages (Open-Meteo).</p>

  <h2>Wildlife calendar, month by month</h2>
  {MONTHS.map((name, i) => (<><h3>{name}</h3><p>{calendar[i]}</p></>))}

  <h2>Planning tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Book cruises early</b> for June–August and the Christmas–New Year holidays, the busiest periods.</li>
    <li><b>Cruise or land-based?</b> Small cruise ships reach the remote western and southern islands; land-based trips from Santa Cruz and San Cristóbal are cheaper and good for people prone to seasickness.</li>
    <li><b>Budget for park fees:</b> since August 2024 the national park entrance fee for most foreign adults is US$200, plus a transit control card bought at the airport in Quito or Guayaquil.</li>
    <li><b>Bring a wetsuit or rent one</b> in the cool season, and pack seasickness tablets for open-sea crossings.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Combine with <a href="/destinations/quito/">Quito</a> — see <a href="/best-time-to-visit-ecuador/">the best time to visit Ecuador</a>.</p>
</Guide>
